import{_ as a,o as r,c as s,a0 as t}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Language servers are lazy, per root, and never shared","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0043-language-servers-are-lazy-per-root-and-never-shared.md","filePath":"adr/0043-language-servers-are-lazy-per-root-and-never-shared.md","lastUpdated":1792093740000}'),o={name:"adr/0043-language-servers-are-lazy-per-root-and-never-shared.md"};function n(d,e,i,c,l,h){return r(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="language-servers-are-lazy-per-root-and-never-shared" tabindex="-1">Language servers are lazy, per root, and never shared <a class="header-anchor" href="#language-servers-are-lazy-per-root-and-never-shared" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Language servers are lazy, per root, and never shared&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #136 has to decide what a Language server costs. Servers are heavy and slow to warm: rust-analyzer and gopls index for tens of seconds and can hold hundreds of megabytes, and an answer taken mid-index is partial.</p><p><strong>One server per (language, root) pair, started by the first call that needs it, alive for the session, closed idempotently at <code>session_shutdown</code>.</strong> The root is the nearest marker walking up from the file (<code>Cargo.toml</code>, <code>go.mod</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code> or <code>package.json</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>compile_commands.json</code> or <code>compile_flags.txt</code>), falling back to the session root. A <strong>Concurrent server bound</strong> of four servers per session evicts the idlest server and its Open documents. Servers are never spawned for a Dependency source or a Named root, and never spawned when the project is not trusted unless the settings say otherwise.</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> A persistent daemon shared across sessions, which is what the existing agent-LSP products run, survives restarts and shares one index between concurrent agents. It is rejected for v1 because it adds a process this package must find, health-check, version-match, and upgrade; because a server&#39;s state would then outlive the session whose settings, trust decision, and working set produced it; and because the eviction and memory story gets harder when several sessions own one process. Spawning per call is impossible in practice: a real server takes longer to index than the call can wait. Starting every detected language eagerly at <code>session_start</code> makes a C++ session pay for rust-analyzer.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> The first call in a language pays the server&#39;s start and index, so results during that window are labeled partial, and the Post-edit annotation never blocks past a short budget waiting for them. A server that dies is restarted once and then marked dead, with the reason in the tool result and in <code>/lsp</code>. Multi-root workspaces are not used: a monorepo gets one server per package root the agent actually touches, which is the same map code and the only way a workspace with several crates answers against the right package.</p>',5)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
